      </div>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The grant</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">On October 1, 2026, the Supreme Court granted review of whether the detention statute requires holding, during removal proceedings, people who, like Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha, are in the United States and were not admitted. The October 1 order grants review and does not decide the case. The bond statute is the statute under which the government may release someone.<Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="THURSDAY, OCTOBER 1, 2026" /><Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="RHONEY, ACTING DIR. V. BARBOSA DA CUNHA, RICARDO A." /><Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted." /><Cite s="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01" passage="Whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) mandates the detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted." /><Cite s="uscode-2024-1226" passage="may release the alien on" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The detention statute uses two descriptions of the person, an applicant for admission and an alien seeking admission. It tells an immigration officer to detain that person for a removal proceeding when the officer decides the person seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted.<Cite s="uscode-2024-1225" passage="in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Detention statute" when="2024 compilation" where="Inspection of applicants for admission" label="Government Publishing Office">
          <p>in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained<Cite s="uscode-2024-1225" passage="in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="4">The Second Circuit held that Barbosa da Cunha may be an applicant for admission and is not seeking admission, and held that the detention statute does not apply to someone in the United States after entering without inspection and admission who was not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter. The Second Circuit affirmed the district court, which had required a bond hearing or release. A bond hearing is a hearing on release. Release in his case followed a finding that he was not a danger and not a flight risk. The hearing did not itself require release. He has never been arrested for or charged with a crime. The Second Circuit's April 28 opinion calls Barbosa da Cunha the petitioner.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha (“Petitioner”)" /> Under the Department of Homeland Security's July 10 memorandum, an applicant for admission may not be released from custody except by parole. That memorandum says the only people eligible for release on bond are people already admitted.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage={`Petitioner may be an “applicant for admission,” but he is not “seeking admission.”`} /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Section 1225(b)(2)(A) does not apply to noncitizens, such as Petitioner, who are present in the United States after entering the country without inspection and admission" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="presents no danger to persons or property or risk of flight, released him on bond." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="has never been arrested for or charged with a crime." /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="may not be released from DHS custody except by INA § 212(d)(5) parole." /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="The only aliens eligible for a custody determination and release on recognizance, bond, or conditional parole under INA § 236(a) are aliens admitted to the United States" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="U.S. Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit" when="April 28, 2026" where="Opinion" label="Barbosa da Cunha">
          <p>Petitioner may be an “applicant for admission,” but he is not “seeking admission.”<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage={`Petitioner may be an “applicant for admission,” but he is not “seeking admission.”`} /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">Barbosa da Cunha entered around 2005, lived in the United States for more than twenty years, applied for asylum in 2016, and held a work permit. On September 26, 2025, officers arrested him while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts. An immigration judge denied a bond hearing. A district court then ordered a bond hearing or release within ten days, and an immigration judge released him on bond after finding no danger and no flight risk. The question the government put to the Court is whether the detention statute requires holding people in that situation for the removal proceedings. Its memorandum says an applicant for admission may not be released except by parole. By the grant, the dispute was no longer his case alone, or only the Fifth Circuit and the Eighth Circuit, the two appeals courts whose divided panels the April opinion said had agreed with the government.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="lived in the United States for more than twenty years after entering the country" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="applied for asylum in 2016 and was granted a valid work permit." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="arrested Petitioner while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Petitioner requested a bond hearing, which an immigration judge denied based on a determination that he was subject to mandatory detention" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="The district court granted the petition and ordered the government to either provide a bond hearing or release Petitioner within ten days." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="presents no danger to persons or property or risk of flight, released him on bond." /><Cite s="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01" passage="Whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) mandates the detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted." /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="may not be released from DHS custody except by INA § 212(d)(5) parole." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="divided panels in two other circuits have agreed with the government" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Buenrostro-Mendez v. Bondi, 166 F.4th 494, 508 (5th Cir. 2026)" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Avila v. Bondi, 170 F.4th 1128, 1138 (8th Cir. 2026)" /><Cite s="ca4-bond-2026-09-10" passage="The First, Second, Third, Sixth, Seventh, Ninth, Tenth, and Eleventh Circuits concluded that Section 1226 applies to noncitizens inside our country" /><Cite s="ca4-bond-2026-09-10" passage="The Fifth and Eighth Circuits ruled, on the other hand, that Section 1225 applies to all noncitizens who have not been admitted to the United States" /><Cite s="ca4-bond-2026-09-10" passage="they must be detained without bond" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The government's petition asked the Court to wait on this case. It said the Court should hold this petition pending another petition, Lopez-Campos, which it called a "better vehicle" for resolving whether holding those people is lawful, and then dispose of this petition as appropriate. The October 1 order granted this petition.<Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="The Court should hold the petition in this case pending the resolution of the petition in Lopez-Campos and then dispose of this petition as appropriate." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Lopez-Campos is a better vehicle than this case for resolving the lawfulness of applying Section 1225(b)(2)(A) to aliens who are present in the country without having been admitted." /><Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In July 2025 the government changed the executive practice of bond for people present without having been admitted or paroled. The Second Circuit describes it as executive practice over thirty years.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="in July 2025, the government changed that long-settled practice" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Executive Branch practice over thirty years" /><Cite s="fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06" passage="Despite being applicants for admission, aliens who are present without having been admitted or paroled (formerly referred to as aliens who entered without inspection) will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination." /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The Court's order list for Thursday, October 1, 2026, grants the petitions for review in a group that includes Philip L. Rhoney, acting director, against Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha. The petition names Rhoney as Acting Field Office Director of the Buffalo Field Office of Immigration and Customs Enforcement, substituted for his predecessor.<Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="THURSDAY, OCTOBER 1, 2026" /><Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="RHONEY, ACTING DIR. V. BARBOSA DA CUNHA, RICARDO A." /><Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Philip L. Rhoney, Acting Field Office Director of the Buffalo Field Office of U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Acting Field Office Director Rhoney is automatically substituted for his predecessor." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The question the Court agreed to hear asks whether the detention statute requires holding, during removal proceedings, people who are in the United States and were not admitted.<Cite s="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01" passage="present in the United States without having been admitted" /><Cite s="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01" passage="CERT. GRANTED 10/1/2026" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The Court's list of cases set for argument is dated October 1, 2026. This case is on it with a grant date of October 1. That line does not set a day for argument.<Cite s="scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01" passage="As of October 1, 2026" /><Cite s="scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01" passage="RHONEY V. BARBOSA DA CUNHA" /><Cite s="scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01" passage="Granted: 10/1/26" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The docket, opened July 23, 2026, records the petition that day, a response on August 21, a reply on September 4, distribution for the September 28 conference, and a grant on October 1. The same page says the petition and the response were to be filed on paper only.<Cite s="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-02" passage="Docketed: July 23, 2026" /><Cite s="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-02" passage="Petition GRANTED." /><Cite s="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-02" passage="should be submitted in paper form only" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Officers arrested him on September 26, 2025, while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts. A judge then ordered a bond hearing or release, and an immigration judge released him on bond.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="On September 26, 2025, officers from U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Norwood, Massachusetts" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="provide him with a bond hearing or release him" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The coverage</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The catch</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3"><strong>The count</strong>  CBS News wrote that the Court would consider whether the administration can detain tens of thousands of immigrants during removal proceedings without bond hearings. That count is not the question the Court granted. On September 10 the Fourth Circuit wrote that district courts sided with the petitioners in over twenty thousand individual cases. That figure counts cases, not people held.<Cite s="cbs-detention-grant-2026-10-01" passage="detain tens of thousands of immigrants during their removal proceedings without access to bond hearings" /><Cite s="ca4-bond-2026-09-10" passage="sided with the petitioners in over twenty thousand individual cases" /><Cite s="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01" passage="Whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) mandates the detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted." /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="CBS News" when="October 1, 2026" where="Report on the grant" label="CBS News">
          <p>The Supreme Court on Thursday said it will consider whether the Trump administration can detain tens of thousands of immigrants during their removal proceedings without access to bond hearings.<Cite s="cbs-detention-grant-2026-10-01" passage="The Supreme Court on Thursday said it will consider whether the Trump administration can detain tens of thousands of immigrants during their removal proceedings without access to bond hearings." /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>The grant</strong>  The Associated Press wrote that the Court agreed to review a policy denying bond hearings. The Hill wrote that the Supreme Court announced Thursday it will decide the legality of President Trump's mandatory detention policy. The question granted is one statutory question, and the October 1 order grants review of it.<Cite s="ap-detention-grant-2026-10-01" passage="agreed to review a Trump administration policy denying bond hearings" /><Cite s="hill-detention-grant-2026-10-01" passage="announced Thursday it will decide the legality" /><Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>The notice</strong>  Customs and Border Protection's public notice describes mandatory detention for applicants for admission the department encounters for the first time after they fail to lawfully present for inspection at a port of entry. The July 10 memorandum says the first encounter counts regardless of when or where it happens. Barbosa da Cunha was arrested driving to work in Norwood, years after he entered.<Cite s="cbp-detention-notice-2025-09-18" passage="for the first time after failing to lawfully present for inspection at a U.S. port of entry" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="regardless of when or where that first encounter occurs." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="arrested Petitioner while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="lived in the United States for more than twenty years after entering the country" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="where-this-sits">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The practice</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Where this sits</h2>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The 1997 Federal Register rule made people present without having been admitted or paroled eligible for bond.</strong><span>{computed.daysOpinionToGrant} days from the appeals decision to the grant, counted from the dates on those records.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Date", "Record"]} rows={chronology.rows} ariaLabel="Dates from the 1997 bond rule to the grant of review" />
          <p class="chart-source">Dates from the Federal Register rule, the appeals opinion, the Customs notice, the Board decision, the Fourth Circuit opinion, the October 2 docket that records the grant, the rehearing order, and the October 1 order list.<Cite s="fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-notice-2025-09-18" /><Cite s="bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05" /><Cite s="ca4-bond-2026-09-10" /><Cite s="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-02" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25" /><Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" /></p>
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">On March 6, 1997, a Federal Register rule said that people present without having been admitted or paroled would be eligible for bond and bond redetermination. The Second Circuit describes the executive practice that followed as thirty years old. In July 2025 the government changed that practice and said the detention statute, rather than the bond statute, is the detention authority for every applicant for admission. On September 5, 2025, the Board of Immigration Appeals said immigration judges lack authority to grant bond to people present in the United States without admission. On April 28, 2026, the Second Circuit held the opposite for someone already present who was not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter, and it affirmed. On September 25 the full court refused rehearing. Judge Steven J. Menashi dissented and argued that the detention statute applies and that Barbosa da Cunha must be detained without a bond hearing. Judge Matthew A. Schwartz voted to deny rehearing and wrote that he agreed with that dissent. On October 1 the Supreme Court granted review.<Cite s="fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06" passage="Despite being applicants for admission, aliens who are present without having been admitted or paroled (formerly referred to as aliens who entered without inspection) will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Executive Branch practice over thirty years" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="in July 2025, the government changed that long-settled practice" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="section 235 of the Immigration and Nationality Act (INA), rather than section 236, is the applicable immigration detention authority for all applicants for admission." /><Cite s="bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05" passage="Immigration Judges lack authority to hear bond requests or to grant bond to aliens who are present in the United States without admission." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25" passage="rehearing in banc is hereby DENIED." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25" passage="dissents by opinion from the denial of rehearing in banc." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25" passage="Although I agree with the analysis in the dissent, I support the decision to deny rehearing en banc." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25" passage="The government and Judge Menashi in his dissent from the denial of rehearing en banc argue that Section 1225 applies and that Petitioner must be detained without a bond hearing." /><Cite s="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01" passage="The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted." /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Federal Register" when="March 6, 1997" where="Detention rule" label="FR Doc. 97-5250">
          <p>Despite being applicants for admission, aliens who are present without having been admitted or paroled (formerly referred to as aliens who entered without inspection) will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination.<Cite s="fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06" passage="Despite being applicants for admission, aliens who are present without having been admitted or paroled (formerly referred to as aliens who entered without inspection) will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination." /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Judge Matthew A. Schwartz voted to deny rehearing and wrote that he agreed with Judge Menashi's dissent, the argument that the detention statute applies and that Barbosa da Cunha must be detained without a bond hearing.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25" passage="Although I agree with the analysis in the dissent, I support the decision to deny rehearing en banc." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25" passage="The government and Judge Menashi in his dissent from the denial of rehearing en banc argue that Section 1225 applies and that Petitioner must be detained without a bond hearing." /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Judge Matthew A. Schwartz" when="September 25, 2026" where="Concurrence in the denial of rehearing" label="Second Circuit">
          <p>Although I agree with the analysis in the dissent, I support the decision to deny rehearing en banc.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25" passage="Although I agree with the analysis in the dissent, I support the decision to deny rehearing en banc." /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <SourcedBlock source="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On April 28, 2026, the Second Circuit affirmed the district court. It held that detention of a person in the United States after entering without inspection and admission, who was not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter, follows the bond statute, not mandatory detention. Judge Cabranes agreed and filed a separate opinion. The appeal was argued April 6.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Decided: April 28, 2026" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Argued: April 6, 2026" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Judge Cabranes concurs in the judgment and opinion of the Court and files a separate opinion." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" kind="record" detail>
          <p>In July 2025 the government changed its practice. The opinion cites immigration enforcement guidance dated July 8, 2025. The petition names that guidance as Todd M. Lyons, Acting Director of Immigration and Customs Enforcement, and says applicants for admission may not be released from custody except by parole. Divided panels in the Fifth and Eighth Circuits agreed with the government.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Interim Guidance Regarding Detention Authority for Applicants for Admission (July 8, 2025)" /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Todd M. Lyons, Acting Director of U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement, Interim Guidance Regarding Detention Authority for Applicants for Admission (July 8, 2025)." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="may not be released from ICE custody” except by “parole”" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Buenrostro-Mendez v. Bondi, 166 F.4th 494, 508 (5th Cir. 2026)" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Avila v. Bondi, 170 F.4th 1128, 1138 (8th Cir. 2026)" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On September 25, 2026, the Second Circuit denied rehearing by the full court. Judges Bianco and Nathan, joined by four others, concurred in that denial. Judge Menashi dissented.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25" passage="rehearing in banc is hereby DENIED." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25" passage="dissents by opinion from the denial of rehearing in banc." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On September 5, 2025, the Board of Immigration Appeals held that immigration judges lack authority to hear bond requests, or to grant bond, for people present in the United States without admission.<Cite s="bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05" passage="Decided September 5, 2025" /><Cite s="bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05" passage="lack authority to hear bond requests or to grant bond to aliens who are present in the United States without admission." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The signed memorandum, a one-page scan, is addressed from Commissioner Rodney S. Scott. It says the department's position is that applicants for admission are subject to mandatory detention and may not be released except by parole.<Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="FROM: Rodney S. Scott" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="SUBJECT: Detention of Applicants for Admission" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="subject to mandatory detention under INA" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="unknowns">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="6">This case has a grant date of October 1 and no argument day on the list of cases set for argument. The October 2 docket's last disposition is the grant, and later filings go through the electronic system. The question the Court accepted is the statute. The government's petition says a petition from the Sixth Circuit in Lopez-Campos also presents whether applying the detention statute comports with due process. The Second Circuit said it need not rely on constitutional avoidance, the practice of reading a statute so as not to decide a constitutional problem. The petition says the Second Circuit did not reach a standalone holding on the constitutionality of detaining him without a bond hearing. A petition from the Fifth Circuit in Buenrostro-Mendez was distributed for the September 28 conference, then rescheduled, and those proceedings list no grant. The Sixth Circuit petition was distributed for that same conference, and its proceedings list no grant.<Cite s="scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01" passage="RHONEY V. BARBOSA DA CUNHA" /><Cite s="scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01" passage="Granted: 10/1/26" /><Cite s="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-02" passage="Petition GRANTED." /><Cite s="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-02" passage="Subsequent party and amicus filings in the case should now be submitted through" /><Cite s="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01" passage="Whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) mandates the detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="In addition to the statutory question presented in this case, Lopez-Campos also squarely presents the question whether applying Section 1225(b)(2)(A)’s mandatory-detention scheme to such aliens comports with due process." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="we need not rely here upon the doctrine of constitutional avoidance." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="the Second Circuit did not reach a standalone holding on the constitutionality of detaining respondent without a bond hearing" /><Cite s="scotus-docket-lopez-campos-2026-10-03" passage="United States Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit" /><Cite s="scotus-docket-lopez-campos-2026-10-03" passage="DISTRIBUTED for Conference of 9/28/2026." /><Cite s="scotus-docket-buenrostro-2026-10-03" passage="United States Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit" /><Cite s="scotus-docket-buenrostro-2026-10-03" passage="DISTRIBUTED for Conference of 9/28/2026." /><Cite s="scotus-docket-buenrostro-2026-10-03" passage="Rescheduled." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-absence>The October 2 docket lists a response filed on August 21 and a reply filed on September 4, and that page does not link either document. The Justice Department file is the petition, not the response or the reply.</p>
        <SourcedBlock source="cbp-detention-notice-2025-09-18" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Customs and Border Protection's public notice says Commissioner Rodney S. Scott issued guidance on July 10, 2025, and that applicants for admission encountering the department for the first time after failing to lawfully present for inspection at a port of entry are subject to mandatory detention.<Cite s="cbp-detention-notice-2025-09-18" passage="on July 10, 2025" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-notice-2025-09-18" passage="subject to mandatory detention under INA Section 235" /></p>
