longstanding agency interpretation. See Bankamerica Corp. v. United
States, 462 U.S. 122, 131 (1983) (“[T]he mere failure of administrative
agencies to act is in no sense a binding administrative interpretation
that the Government lacks the authority to act.”) (internal quotation
marks and citation omitted).             But a longstanding, incorrect
interpretation by an agency is more easily explained when the effect
of that interpretation was to aggrandize an agency’s power. See, e.g.,
Pereira v. Sessions, 585 U.S. 198, 202 (2018) (holding that DHS’s notices
to appear were inadequate). We recognize that agencies have strong
incentives to maximize their own power and discretion. See Nat’l
Fed’n of Indep. Bus. v. Dep’t of Lab., Occupational Safety & Health Admin.,
595 U.S. 109, 119 (2022). Accordingly, where an agency has long failed
to reach for a power and only later changes its mind, we may consider
that as persuasive evidence that the power was never conferred in the
first place. See id.
