228-Section 1225(b)(2)(A) does not apply to them. Under this interpretation, an apt analogy would be to a moviegoer who sneaks into the theater without paying for a ticket and watches half the film. The moviegoer cannot be said to be “seeking” to enter the theater when he is already inside. Rather, he is requesting to remain. This is where our sister circuits have split. It is worth spending a moment to tease out their disagreement over how properly to interpret the phrase “applicant for admission.” Section 1225(a)(1) states that all noncitizens “present in the United States who ha[ve] not been admitted or who arrive[ ] in the United States,” whether or not “at a designated port of arrival,” “shall be deemed for purposes of this chapter an applicant for admission.” 8 U.S.C. § 1225(a)(1). The majority of courts have interpreted this phrase as a definitional provision, defining a statutory term of art: “applicant for admission.” See, e.g., Barbosa da Cunha, 175 F.4th at 75; Lopez-Campos, 175 F.4th at 722–23. Both the Government and the Petitioners urge us to adopt this interpretation as well. “When Congress takes the trouble to define the term it uses,” courts are obligated to use that definition rather than the ordinary meaning of the term of art wherever it appears in the text. Dep’t of Agric. Rural Dev. Rural Hous. Serv. v. Kirtz, 601 U.S. 42, 59 (2024). Conversely, where Congress does not define a term of art, we look to the “ordinary, contemporary, common meaning” of the word. Othi v. Holder, 734 F.3d 259, 265 (4th Cir. 2023) (quoting United States v. Powell, 680 F.3d 350, 355 (4th Cir. 2012)). Under this reading, Congress has separately defined the phrase, “applicant for admission,” and the word, “admission.” It did not define the word “seeking.”
