537-No circuit court had opined on the practice.8 Given that legal landscape, when Congress clarified that the Attorney General must take into custody individuals covered by subparagraph (E), its action changed the status quo. At bottom, however, the intent of the 119th Congress when it passed the Laken Riley Act in 2025 cannot tell us the meaning of the detention provision in Section 1225(b)(2)(A), which a different Congress enacted in 1996. See Rodriguez Vazquez v. Bostock, 183 F.4th 1160, 1207–1208 (9th Cir. 2026) (Bea, J., dissenting) (“[I]t is wellestablished that ‘the views of a subsequent Congress form a hazardous basis for inferring the intent of an earlier one.’” (quoting Cipollone v. Liggett Grp., Inc., 505 U.S. 504, 520 (1992))). The fact that the 119th Congress did not undertake a more sweeping revision of Section 1225 or 1226 “to correct the Executive Branch’s misunderstanding,” Maj. Op. 34, tells us only what that Congress intended to accomplish in 2025. It tells us nothing about what Section 1225(b)(2)(A) meant decades earlier when it was enacted, which is the meaning that continues to control until Congress amends it. See Wisc. Cent. Ltd., 585 U.S. at 284 (“Until [Congress] exercises that power, the people may rely on the original meaning of the written law.”); Watson v. Republican Nat’l Comm., 146 S. Ct. 2165, 2189 (2026) (Alito, J., dissenting) (“Statutes enacted decades after the fact are seldom probative” of “what the statute meant at the time it became law.”).
