Audit gaps added in the structure turn. Each line cites a document outside the manifest, with the audit's link. The grade is what the document does for the seven answers. None is graded C for being unpinned. The story turn admits every A and B before it writes. A line's own words are not the page's unknown.

- 1997-03-06, Federal Register detention rule. The record audit cites [the March 6, 1997 Federal Register](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-1997-03-06/pdf/FR-1997-03-06.pdf). It says the rule stated that people present without admission will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination. Not in the manifest. Structure grade A for answer 5: the before-state was written eligibility, not only the Second Circuit's later account of thirty years. The story turn admits the rule before writing that before-state. Search outside the registry if it is not already held: the March 6, 1997 Federal Register rule on bond for people present without admission. Do not carry this line as an absence on the page.
- 2025-07-08, ICE interim guidance. The census found no registry copy. The audit cites [the July 8 ICE memorandum](https://iptp-production.s3.amazonaws.com/media/documents/2025.07.08_ICE_-_Interim_Guidance_Regarding_Detention_Authority_for_Applicants_0JGK0CH.pdf) and says it makes this group ineligible for a bond hearing before an immigration judge. The July 10 Customs memorandum already says section 235, not section 236, applies to all applicants for admission and that release is by parole only, and the Board decision says immigration judges lack bond authority. Structure grade D. The pins already change the answers this memorandum would change.
- 2026, Eighth Circuit opinion in Avila. The audit cites [the opinion](https://ecf.ca8.uscourts.gov/opndir/26/03/253248P.pdf) for calling the relationship of "applicant for admission" and "seeking admission" the central inquiry. The Second Circuit pin already states both phrases and says the Eighth Circuit agreed with the government. Structure grade D.
- 2026-09-10, Fourth Circuit opinion. The audit cites [the opinion](https://www.ca4.uscourts.gov/opinions/257044.P.pdf). It describes nine other circuits against the government and "over twenty thousand individual cases," which the audit says are the court's descriptions, not a count of people detained. Structure grade A for answers 2 and 3: the dispute is wider than the two circuits named in the April opinion, and that case count is not CBS's count of people. The story turn admits the opinion before writing that width.
- 2026, government petition in 26-104. The docket required paper filing. The audit cites the [Justice Department copy](https://www.justice.gov/d9/2026-07/barbosa_da_cunha_hold_pet_-_final.pdf). It says the petition treats a companion case, Lopez-Campos, as also presenting whether the detention comports with due process, and the granted question does not include that. The same copy is a petition a reader can open even though the docket does not link one. Structure grade B for answer 6. The story turn admits it before writing what this grant does not decide.
- 2026, paper response and reply in 26-104. The audit says it could not read them in full and gives no separate public URL. Structure grade B for answer 6: the parties' arguments to the Court are not in the admitted record. The story turn looks for a public copy outside the registry before the page states that absence. This line does not become the page's sentence.
- 2026-09-25, full Second Circuit rehearing opinions. The pinned file is the three-page order. The audit cites [the complete file](https://ww3.ca2.uscourts.gov/decisions/OPN/25-3141_complete%20en%20banc_opn.pdf) and quotes Judge Schwartz that he agrees with the analysis in the dissent and supports denying rehearing. Structure grade B for answer 5. The story turn admits the full file before writing that concurrence.
- 2026, Fifth Circuit opinion in Buenrostro-Mendez. The audit cites [the opinion](https://www.macarthurjustice.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Buenrostro-opinion-02.06.2026.pdf) as accepting the government's reading. The Second Circuit pin already says that divided panel agreed with the government. Structure grade D.
- Supreme Court dockets [25-1415](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/25-1415.html) and [26-43](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/26-43.html). The audit says petitions from the Sixth and Fifth Circuit rulings were also before the Court and does not say how they were disposed. Structure grade B for answer 6. The story turn checks those dockets before the page treats this grant as the only petition on the question.
- No detention-population series. The audit could not separate people held under this reading from all ICE detainees and from habeas filings. CBS's "tens of thousands" and the Fourth Circuit's case count are not substitutes for each other. Structure grade B for answer 3. There is no document to admit. The story does not carry a population count.
- District court opinion. The audit says the Justice Department petition's appendix includes it. The arrest, the ten-day order, and the bond release are already stated in the Second Circuit opinion. Structure grade D.

