checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.log:212:## 4. Critical and Major Findings
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.log:10904:- **Major, grade A: The legal hinge.** The page says the Court will decide whether the statute requires holding people “who are in the United States and were not admitted.” The dispute turns on two different phrases: **“applicant for admission”** and **“seeking admission.”** The [Eighth Circuit opinion](https://ecf.ca8.uscourts.gov/opndir/26/03/253248P.pdf) calls their relationship “the central inquiry”; the [Fourth Circuit opinion](https://www.ca4.uscourts.gov/opinions/257044.P.pdf) explains that its petitioners contested the second condition. The correct account is that the government treats the phrases as equivalent, while the Second Circuit held that someone arrested in the interior long after entry is not then seeking admission.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.log:10906:- **Major, grade A: The conflict is nationwide.** The page says only that “Divided panels in the Fifth and Eighth Circuits agreed with the government.” The [Fourth Circuit’s September 10 opinion](https://www.ca4.uscourts.gov/opinions/257044.P.pdf) identifies nine other circuits on the opposite side and says “over five hundred” district judges had rejected the government’s reading in “over twenty thousand individual cases.” Those are the court’s descriptions, not independently audited counts of people detained. The correct account is a broad circuit split and a large volume of litigation, rather than a disagreement confined to the three circuits named on the page.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.log:10908:- **Major, grade B: What the 2025 change displaced.** The page says, “In July 2025 the government changed its practice.” The [1997 Federal Register rule](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-1997-03-06/pdf/FR-1997-03-06.pdf) expressly said that people present without admission “will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination.” The [July 8 ICE guidance](https://iptp-production.s3.amazonaws.com/media/documents/2025.07.08_ICE_-_Interim_Guidance_Regarding_Detention_Authority_for_Applicants_0JGK0CH.pdf) instead says they are ineligible for a bond hearing before an immigration judge. The correct account is a reversal of a long-used custody practice through a new executive interpretation of the existing statute.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.log:10910:- **Major, grade B: The grant leaves a constitutional question open.** The page says, “The question the Court agreed to hear asks whether the detention statute requires holding” this group. That is true, but the [government’s petition](https://www.justice.gov/d9/2026-07/barbosa_da_cunha_hold_pet_-_final.pdf) says a companion case, *Lopez-Campos*, “also squarely presents” whether that detention comports with due process. The [granted question](https://www.supremecourt.gov/qp/26-00104qp.pdf) names only 8 U.S.C. § 1225(b)(2)(A). The correct account is that review has been granted on the statutory question; the separate constitutional challenge remains unresolved by this grant.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.log:10912:- **Major, grade B: The person and remedy at the center of the case.** The page says officers arrested Barbosa da Cunha “while he was driving to work” and that an immigration judge “released him on bond.” The pinned [Second Circuit opinion](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt:155) also says he had lived in the country for over twenty years, had work authorization and an asylum application under review, and that the government conceded he was neither a danger nor a flight risk. The correct account should explain that the disputed hearing is a chance to argue for release, **not automatic release**. His hearing resulted in bond; removal proceedings were still pending.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.log:10922:## 4. Critical and Major Findings
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.log:10926:| **Major, A: statutory hinge** | “present in the United States and were not admitted” | The [Eighth Circuit](https://ecf.ca8.uscourts.gov/opndir/26/03/253248P.pdf) identifies whether an “applicant for admission” is also “seeking admission” as “the central inquiry.” That distinction determines which detention provision governs. | Explain both phrases and the competing readings in the main story view. |
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.log:10927:| **Major, A: national reach** | “Divided panels in the Fifth and Eighth Circuits agreed with the government.” | The [Fourth Circuit](https://www.ca4.uscourts.gov/opinions/257044.P.pdf) lists nine circuits reaching the contrary statutory result and describes “over twenty thousand individual cases.” A reader otherwise cannot see why Supreme Court review matters nationally. | State the split and label the case figure as a court-reported litigation count, not a detention-population count. |
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.log:10928:| **Major, B: prior rule and policy change** | “In July 2025 the government changed its practice.” | The [1997 rule](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-1997-03-06/pdf/FR-1997-03-06.pdf) expressly preserved bond eligibility for people present without admission. The [ICE guidance](https://iptp-production.s3.amazonaws.com/media/documents/2025.07.08_ICE_-_Interim_Guidance_Regarding_Detention_Authority_for_Applicants_0JGK0CH.pdf) removed bond hearings under the agency’s new reading. That contrast answers *what changed*. | Put the earlier rule beside the 2025 guidance. |
